\subsection*{4.7 Conclusion: Toward Ethical Agency}
Simulated agency in RSVP is a sparse, variational, and categorical process that realizes autonomy as an \emph{ongoing alignment} with coherence potentials rather than as domination by external objectives. HYDRA and TARTAN show how such agency scales across collectives without forfeiting local control. In the chapters that follow we turn to entropic economics and governance, where these same principles provide design criteria for incentive structures and institutions that preserve meaning under resource constraints \citep{hanson2003, ortega1957}.
\section*{Chapter 5: Entropic Economics and the Cognitive Commons}
\subsection*{5.1 Introduction: Reimagining Economics through Entropy}
Classical political economy treats attention as a scarce, tradable asset and proceeds to
optimize its capture. As argued in Chapter~1, this logic fuels infrastructures that
commoditize cognition and engineer funnels toward platform revenue \citep{zuboff2019}.
In the RSVP paradigm, attention is not merely a stock to be seized but a \emph{flow}
emergent from field interactions: coherence (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Î¦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Î¦</span></span></span></span>), directionality (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4444em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span></span></span></span>), and
uncertainty (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span>). Entropic economics reframes coordination as a problem of
\emph{field alignment} within a cognitive commons, rather than as a zero-sum contest
over a fixed pie \citep{hanson2003, lanier2018}.
This chapter develops the formal substrate for such an economy. We model attention
allocation inside the fibered symmetric monoidal <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">âˆž</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord">âˆž</span></span></span></span>-category
<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="script">C</mi><mtext>RSVP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\text{RSVP}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.05834em;">C</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0583em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord text mtight"><span class="mord mtight">RSVP</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (Chapter~3), use sheaf theory to track local/global
